    {
      "type": "h2",
      "text": "FAQs"
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "1. Can Invisalign fix severely crooked teeth?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Invisalign works well for mild to moderate misalignment. But for severely crooked teeth or complex bite issues, traditional braces or other advanced orthodontic treatment options may be more effective and reliable for your orthodontic needs."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "2. Are braces more painful than Invisalign?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Braces can cause soreness, especially after wire tightening. Invisalign aligners, made from smooth plastic, feel more comfortable and cause less gum irritation during the tooth movement process for most Invisalign patients."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "3. Do dental insurance plans cover Invisalign?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Yes, many dental insurance plans cover part of Invisalign aligners cost. Coverage depends on your specific policy and whether it includes orthodontic treatment options like clear aligners or conventional braces."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "4. Can I eat everything with Invisalign?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "Absolutely. Just remove the Invisalign trays before eating. Unlike braces, there are no food restrictions for eating patients. You can enjoy your favourites and clean your teeth before popping the trays back in."
    },
    {
      "type": "h3",
      "text": "5. How often do I visit the orthodontist with braces vs Invisalign?"
    },
    {
      "type": "p",
      "text": "With traditional metal braces, visits are usually monthly for adjustments. Invisalign treatment typically requires fewer check-ins, depending on your progress and how closely you follow the treatment plan."
    }
